# Introduction to Solid State Physics (Degen, HS26): Weekly Problem Sets 7-9. Sets 7 and 8 are condensed from the official
# sheets and solutions. Solution Set 9 was not released at the time of writing (2026-11-16): the solution of sheet 9 is our own,
# checked against the script (4.1, 4.2.5) and numerically in game/test/sheets-check-solid-state.mjs. Replace it when the
# official one appears.
# Style rule for these files: no ASCII apostrophes inside r'...' strings (use ’ in text, ^\prime in math).
from ss_sheet_lib import S, Q, F

EX = lambda n, q: {'file': f'Exercises/Weekly Problem Set {n}.pdf', 'where': f'Weekly Problem Set {n}, Exercise {q}; official solution: Weekly Solution Set {n}.pdf'}
OWN = lambda n, q: {'file': f'Exercises/Weekly Problem Set {n}.pdf', 'where': f'Weekly Problem Set {n}, Exercise {q}; the official solution was not released yet (16 Nov 2026): this solution is our own, following script 4.1 (eqs. 4.1–4.9) and 4.2.5'}

S(7, 'Fermi surface, electrons versus phonons, tight binding with next-nearest neighbours', '2026-11-06', [
 Q(1, 'Fermi surface in two dimensions', 'fermi-sea', ['ss-fer-L1', 'ss-fer-L2', 'ss-fer-D1', 'ss-fer-b1'],
   r'A rectangular lattice with $a_1=2\pi$ Å and $a_2=\pi$ Å, one atom per lattice site, three valence electrons per atom that move like free electrons. Draw the Fermi surface in the reduced zone scheme.',
   [(r'Electron density and $k_F$.', r'$n_e=\frac{3}{a_1a_2}=\frac{3}{2\pi^2}$ Å$^{-2}$. Counting: $N_e=\pi k_F^2\cdot\frac{A}{(2\pi)^2}\cdot2=\frac{Ak_F^2}{2\pi}$, so $k_F=\sqrt{2\pi n_e}=\sqrt{3/\pi}$ Å$^{-1}\approx0.98$ Å$^{-1}$.'),
    (r'Reciprocal lattice and zones.', r'$b_1=\frac{2\pi}{a_1}=1$ Å$^{-1}$, $b_2=\frac{2\pi}{a_2}=2$ Å$^{-1}$. First zone: the rectangle $|k_1|\le0.5$, $|k_2|\le1$ Å$^{-1}$ (area 2 Å$^{-2}$ = room for 2 electrons per atom). Three electrons fill an area of 1.5 zones. The Fermi circle ($k_F=0.98$) crosses the two long zone edges at $k_1=\pm0.5$ but does not reach $k_2=\pm1$, and the corners (distance $\sqrt{0.5^2+1^2}=1.12>k_F$) stay empty.'),
    (r'Fold back.', F('s7q1-fermi-surface', 'Top: reciprocal lattice with the Fermi circle drawn over the first Brillouin zone (rectangle) and the second zone around it. Bottom: the occupied parts of band 1 and band 2 folded into the first zone') + r'<br>Band 1 (first zone): occupied except for the regions outside the circle near the four corners, i.e. the Fermi line consists of two arcs near the short edges. Band 2: the two circle segments that stick out beyond $k_1=\pm0.5$, shifted by $\mp b_1$ into the first zone, form two half-lenses attached to the opposite long edges. The circle does not reach the third zone. Two partly filled bands: a metal.')],
   [r'$k_F=\sqrt{2\pi n_e}=\sqrt{3/\pi}$ Å$^{-1}$', r'$b_1=1$, $b_2=2$ Å$^{-1}$; zone area holds 2 electrons per atom', r'circle crosses $k_1=\pm0.5$ only; corners of zone 1 empty', r'folded pieces: first band nearly full, second band two segments'], source=EX(7, 1)),
 Q(2, 'Energy of electrons and phonons in solids', 'fermi-sea', ['ss-fer-L1', 'ss-fer-c1', 'ss-pho-c2', 'ss-pho-c4', 'ss-sem-L1'],
   r'Sodium: BCC, $a=4.23$ Å, one valence electron. (a) Estimate the maximum wave vector and energy of a phonon (sound velocity 1000 m/s). (b) The same for an electron. (c) In the band structure, can a phonon excite an electron from the minimum $\Gamma_1$ into the minimum $F_3$? In what form could the energy be supplied instead?<br>' + F('s7q2-na-bands', 'Band structure of sodium (Ching and Callaway 1975): energy in Rydberg against wave vector along Gamma, N, P, H; the lowest band starts at Gamma 1 below the Fermi level, the minimum F3 lies about 0.9 Ry higher near P'),
   [(r'(a) Phonon.', F('s7q2-bz-h-point', 'First Brillouin zone of the BCC lattice (rhombic dodecahedron) with the point H marked on the cube axis', 260) + r'<br>The largest phonon wave vector is at the zone boundary. The reciprocal lattice is FCC with cube edge $4\pi/a$ (sheet 2 Q1); the farthest point on a cube axis is H, at half the cube edge: $k_D=\frac{2\pi}{a}=1.49\times10^{10}\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$. Debye: $\omega_D=k_Dv=1.49\times10^{13}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$, $\hbar\omega_D\approx9.8$ meV.'),
    (r'(b) Electron.', r'$n=\frac{2\cdot1}{a^3}$ (2 atoms per cube, one electron each) $=2.64\times10^{28}\ \mathrm{m^{-3}}$. $k_F=(3\pi^2n)^{1/3}=9.2\times10^9\ \mathrm{m^{-1}}$ (script eq. 3.22), $E_F=\frac{\hbar^2k_F^2}{2m}\approx3.2$ eV.'),
    (r'(c) Can a phonon do it?', r'The transition $\Gamma_1\to F_3$ needs a change of wave vector <b>and</b> of energy (an indirect transition). A phonon has plenty of wave vector ($k_D>k_F$) but only $\sim10$ meV, more than two orders of magnitude below electronic energies of several eV: it changes the electron’s $\mathbf k$ a lot and its energy hardly at all. So no. The energy has to come from a photon (which in turn carries almost no wave vector): in an indirect transition the photon supplies the energy and a phonon the momentum.')],
   [r'$k_D=2\pi/a$ at the H point, $\hbar\omega_D\approx10$ meV', r'$n=2/a^3$, $k_F=(3\pi^2n)^{1/3}=9.2\times10^9$ m$^{-1}$', r'$E_F\approx3.2$ eV', r'phonon: large $k$, tiny energy; photon: large energy, tiny $k$'], source=EX(7, 2)),
 Q(3, 'Band structure of a monatomic chain with nearest- and next-nearest-neighbour hopping', 'fermi-sea', ['ss-fer-L2', 'ss-fer-c3', 'ss-sem-c3', 'ss-sem-L1'],
   r'1D tight-binding chain: onsite energy $E_0$, $\langle n|\hat H|n+1\rangle=-t$, and in addition $\langle n|\hat H|n+2\rangle=-t^\prime\ne0$. (a) Do you expect $t^\prime<t$? Model the atoms as delta wells $\hat V_n=V_0\delta(x-na)$ with orbitals $\Psi_n(x)\propto e^{-|x-na|/d}$ and estimate $t^\prime/t$. (b) Write the equation for the amplitudes $\phi_n$ of $|\Psi\rangle=\sum_n\phi_n|n\rangle$. (c) Solve with $\phi_n=e^{ikna}\phi_0$: $E(k)$. (d) Effective mass $m^*(k)=\hbar^2\left[\frac{\partial^2E}{\partial k^2}\right]^{-1}$.',
   [(r'(a) Size of $t^\prime$.', r'As in script 3.2.3 and sheet 3 Q3: $-t=\langle n|\hat V_n|n+1\rangle\propto\int e^{-|x-na|/d}\delta(x-na)e^{-|x-(n+1)a|/d}dx=e^{-a/d}$ and $-t^\prime\propto e^{-2a/d}$. So $t^\prime/t\approx e^{-a/d}\ll1$ when the orbitals are well localised ($d\ll a$): $t^\prime<t$, and hopping falls off exponentially with distance.'),
    (r'(b) Equation of motion.', r'Project $\hat H|\Psi\rangle=E|\Psi\rangle$ on $\langle n|$ (orthonormal orbitals): $E\phi_n=\sum_m\phi_m\langle n|\hat H|m\rangle=E_0\phi_n-t(\phi_{n-1}+\phi_{n+1})-t^\prime(\phi_{n-2}+\phi_{n+2})$.'),
    (r'(c) Dispersion.', r'$E(k)=E_0-2t\cos(ka)-2t^\prime\cos(2ka)$.'),
    (r'(d) Effective mass.', r'$\frac{\partial^2E}{\partial k^2}=2a^2t\cos(ka)+8a^2t^\prime\cos(2ka)$, so $m^*(k)=\frac{\hbar^2}{2a^2}\frac{1}{t\cos(ka)+4t^\prime\cos(2ka)}$. At $k=0$: $m^*=\frac{\hbar^2}{2a^2(t+4t^\prime)}$, lighter than without $t^\prime$; at the zone boundary $m^*=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2a^2(t-4t^\prime)}$, negative for $t^\prime<t/4$: a hole-like band top.')],
   [r'$t\propto e^{-a/d}$, $t^\prime\propto e^{-2a/d}$', r'$E\phi_n=E_0\phi_n-t(\phi_{n\pm1})-t^\prime(\phi_{n\pm2})$', r'$E(k)=E_0-2t\cos ka-2t^\prime\cos2ka$', r'$m^*=\frac{\hbar^2}{2a^2[t\cos ka+4t^\prime\cos2ka]}$'], source=EX(7, 3)),
])

S(8, 'Repetition week: Miller planes, bulk modulus of argon, photons in a box', '2026-11-13', [
 Q(1, 'Miller planes and reciprocal lattice vectors', 'lattice-gate', ['ss-lat-L1', 'ss-lat-D1', 'ss-lat-Q1', 'ss-lat-c1'],
   r'A family of Miller planes $(hkl)$. (a) Prove that $\mathbf G=h\mathbf a^*+k\mathbf b^*+l\mathbf c^*$ is perpendicular to the plane $(hkl)$. (b) Show that neighbouring planes are $d=2\pi/|\mathbf G_{\min}|$ apart ($\mathbf G_{\min}$: shortest reciprocal lattice vector perpendicular to the planes). (c) With the BCC reciprocal vectors $\mathbf a^*=\frac{2\pi}a(\hat{\mathbf y}+\hat{\mathbf z})$, $\mathbf b^*=\frac{2\pi}a(\hat{\mathbf x}+\hat{\mathbf z})$, $\mathbf c^*=\frac{2\pi}a(\hat{\mathbf x}+\hat{\mathbf y})$ find the family of planes with the highest density of lattice points $\rho=d/V$ ($V$: primitive cell volume). (d) Sketch such a plane in the BCC structure.',
   [(r'(a) Perpendicular.', r'One $(hkl)$ plane cuts the axes at $\mathbf a/h$, $\mathbf b/k$, $\mathbf c/l$. In-plane vectors: $\mathbf v_1=\mathbf a/h-\mathbf b/k$, $\mathbf v_2=\mathbf a/h-\mathbf c/l$. With $\mathbf a^*\cdot\mathbf a=2\pi$, $\mathbf a^*\cdot\mathbf b=0$, ... (script eq. 1.8): $\mathbf G\cdot\mathbf v_1=\frac{h}{h}2\pi-\frac kk2\pi=0$, likewise $\mathbf G\cdot\mathbf v_2=0$. This holds for any lattice, not only cubic ones (compare sheet 2 Q2).'),
    (r'(b) Spacing.', r'$\hat{\mathbf n}=\mathbf G/|\mathbf G|$. One plane goes through the origin, the next through $\mathbf a/h$: $d=\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\frac{\mathbf a}{h}=\frac{h\,2\pi}{h|\mathbf G|}=\frac{2\pi}{|\mathbf G|}$ (for $h,k,l$ without a common factor, which is $\mathbf G_{\min}$).'),
    (r'(c) Densest planes of BCC.', r'$\rho=d/V$ is largest for the largest $d$, i.e. the shortest $\mathbf G$. All three primitive reciprocal vectors have the same length $\frac{2\pi}{a}\sqrt2$, and no integer combination is shorter: $(hkl)=(\pm1,0,0),(0,\pm1,0),(0,0,\pm1)$ with respect to the <b>primitive</b> BCC vectors. Then $d=\frac{a}{\sqrt2}$ and with $V=\frac{a^3}{2}$: $\rho=\frac{\sqrt2}{a^2}$. In the indices of the conventional cube these are the $\{110\}$ planes ($\mathbf a^*\parallel(0,1,1)$ and so on).'),
    (r'(d) Sketch.', F('s8q1-bcc-110', 'BCC cube with the diagonal (110) plane shaded: it contains four corner atoms and the body-centre atom', 240) + r'<br>The normal points along $\mathbf a^*$, $\mathbf b^*$ or $\mathbf c^*$, e.g. $(\hat{\mathbf x}+\hat{\mathbf y})$: the diagonal plane through two opposite cube edges, which also contains the body-centre atom. (Check: rectangle $a\times\sqrt2a$ with $4\cdot\frac14+1=2$ points, $\rho=\frac{2}{\sqrt2a^2}=\frac{\sqrt2}{a^2}$.)')],
   [r'$\mathbf G\cdot\mathbf v_{1,2}=0$ with $\mathbf a^*\cdot\mathbf a=2\pi$', r'$d=2\pi/|\mathbf G|$ by projecting $\mathbf a/h$', r'shortest $\mathbf G$: the primitive reciprocal vectors, $|\mathbf G|=2\sqrt2\pi/a$', r'$\{110\}$ planes of the conventional cube, $\rho=\sqrt2/a^2$'], source=EX(8, 1)),
 Q(2, 'Bulk modulus of solid argon', 'bonding-grotto', ['ss-bon-L1', 'ss-bon-D1', 'ss-bon-c1', 'ss-bon-b1'],
   r'Solid argon: FCC with $a=5.25$ Å (conventional cube edge), Lennard-Jones potential $\phi(r)=4U_0\left[\left(\frac\sigma r\right)^{12}-\left(\frac\sigma r\right)^{6}\right]$. (a) Nearest-neighbour distance? (b) What is $\sigma$? (c) Total energy per atom $u$ (count the bonds). (d) Bulk modulus $K=\Omega_0\left(\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial\Omega^2}\right)_{\Omega_0}$ ($\Omega$: volume per atom) for $U_0/k_B=163$ K.',
   [(r'(a) Spacing.', r'$r_0=\frac a{\sqrt2}\approx3.712$ Å (corner to face centre).'),
    (r'(b) $\sigma$.', r'Equilibrium: $\frac{d\phi}{dr}\Big|_{r_0}=0\Rightarrow12\left(\frac{\sigma}{r_0}\right)^{11}-6\left(\frac\sigma{r_0}\right)^5=0$, $\sigma=\frac{r_0}{2^{1/6}}\approx3.307$ Å. (This uses nearest neighbours only, as the official solution does.)'),
    (r'(c) Energy per atom.', F('s8q2-fcc-neighbours', 'FCC cube with the bonds from one atom to its nearest neighbours drawn', 180) + r'<br>12 nearest neighbours, each bond shared by two atoms: $u=12\cdot\frac12\cdot4U_0\left[\left(\frac\sigma r\right)^{12}-\left(\frac\sigma r\right)^6\right]=24U_0\left[\left(\frac\sigma r\right)^{12}-\left(\frac\sigma r\right)^6\right]$.'),
    (r'(d) Bulk modulus.', r'Four atoms per cube: $\Omega=\frac{a^{\prime3}}4$ with $a^\prime=\sqrt2r$, so $\Omega=\frac{r^3}{\sqrt2}$, $r^6=2\Omega^2$, $r^{12}=4\Omega^4$: $u=24U_0\left[\frac{\sigma^{12}}{4\Omega^4}-\frac{\sigma^6}{2\Omega^2}\right]$. $\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial\Omega^2}=24U_0\left[\frac{5\sigma^{12}}{\Omega^6}-\frac{3\sigma^6}{\Omega^4}\right]$. At equilibrium $\sigma^6=\frac{r_0^6}{2}=\Omega_0^2$: $K=24U_0\left[\frac{5}{\Omega_0}-\frac3{\Omega_0}\right]=\frac{48U_0}{\Omega_0}$. Numbers: $\Omega_0=\frac{a^3}{4}=3.62\times10^{-29}\ \mathrm{m^3}$, $U_0=163\,k_B=2.25\times10^{-21}$ J: $K\approx3.0\times10^9$ Pa $=3.0$ GPa. (The official solution stops at the general expression; its last printed line has a typo in the second coefficient, the result above is checked numerically.)')],
   [r'$r_0=a/\sqrt2$', r'$\sigma=r_0/2^{1/6}=3.31$ Å', r'$u=24U_0[(\sigma/r)^{12}-(\sigma/r)^6]$: 12 neighbours, factor $\frac12$', r'$\Omega=r^3/\sqrt2$ and two derivatives', r'$K=48U_0/\Omega_0\approx3$ GPa'], source=EX(8, 2)),
 Q(3, 'Photons in a box', 'degenerate-core', ['ss-deg-D1', 'ss-deg-c5', 'ss-pho-L2', 'ss-deg-Q1'],
   r'A metal box of side $L$ with photons in thermal equilibrium at $T$; $E=\hbar ck$. (a) Density of states at energy $E$ (two polarisations). (b) Total energy of all photons with Bose–Einstein statistics. (c) Heat capacity of the photon gas.',
   [(r'(a) Density of states.', r'$N(k)=\frac{2V}{(2\pi)^3}\frac43\pi k^3$, with $k=\frac{E}{\hbar c}$: $N(E)=\frac{2V}{(2\pi)^3}\frac{4\pi E^3}{3c^3\hbar^3}$ and $\nu(E)=\frac{dN}{dE}=\frac{VE^2}{\pi^2c^3\hbar^3}$.'),
    (r'(b) Energy.', r'$U=\int_0^\infty dE\,E\,\nu(E)\frac1{e^{E/k_BT}-1}=\frac{Vk_B^4T^4}{\pi^2c^3\hbar^3}\int_0^\infty\frac{y^3}{e^y-1}dy=\frac{\pi^2Vk_B^4T^4}{15c^3\hbar^3}$ ($y=E/k_BT$, integral $=\pi^4/15$). Unlike Debye there is no cutoff: the integral can be done at every $T$. This is the Stefan–Boltzmann law.'),
    (r'(c) Heat capacity.', r'$c_V=\left(\frac{\partial U}{\partial T}\right)_V=\frac{4\pi^2Vk_B^4T^3}{15c^3\hbar^3}\propto T^3$ at all temperatures. Phonons obey the same law only for $T\ll\Theta_D$ (with $c\to v_s$ and 3 polarisations instead of 2).')],
   [r'$\nu(E)=\frac{VE^2}{\pi^2c^3\hbar^3}$ with 2 polarisations', r'$U=\int E\,\nu(E)\,n_B\,dE$, substitution $y=E/k_BT$', r'$U=\frac{\pi^2Vk_B^4T^4}{15c^3\hbar^3}$', r'$c_V=\frac{4\pi^2Vk_B^4T^3}{15c^3\hbar^3}$, no cutoff'], source=EX(8, 3)),
])

S(9, 'Drude model: temperature dependence, two kinds of charge carriers', '2026-11-20', [
 Q(1, 'The temperature dependence of resistance in the Drude model', 'drift-corridor', ['ss-dri-L1', 'ss-dri-L2', 'ss-dri-c1', 'ss-dri-c2', 'ss-dri-Q1'],
   r'Drude assumed the scattering time is set by the thermal speed of a classical gas, $v=\sqrt{3k_BT/m}$. Copper: density 8960 kg/m$^3$, 63.55 g/mol, $\rho=1/\sigma=1.68\times10^{-8}\ \Omega$m at room temperature, one free electron per atom. (a) Calculate the Drude scattering time $\tau$ at room temperature. (b) With the rms speed, estimate the mean free path $\lambda$. (c) Assume $\lambda$ does not depend on $T$: how does $\rho$ scale with $T$? (d) Compare with $\rho(T)$ when the scattering is due to phonons.',
   [(r'(a) Scattering time.', r'$n=\frac{\rho_mN_A}{M}=\frac{8960\cdot6.022\times10^{23}}{0.06355}=8.49\times10^{28}\ \mathrm{m^{-3}}$. From $\sigma=\frac{ne^2\tau}{m}$ (script eq. 4.4): $\tau=\frac{m}{ne^2\rho}=\frac{9.11\times10^{-31}}{8.49\times10^{28}\cdot(1.602\times10^{-19})^2\cdot1.68\times10^{-8}}=2.5\times10^{-14}$ s.'),
    (r'(b) Mean free path.', r'$v=\sqrt{\frac{3k_BT}{m}}=1.17\times10^5$ m/s at 300 K, $\lambda=v\tau\approx2.9$ nm: about ten lattice constants. (With the correct speed, $v_F=1.57\times10^6$ m/s for copper, the same $\tau$ gives $\lambda\approx39$ nm.)'),
    (r'(c) Temperature dependence.', r'$\tau=\lambda/v$, so $\rho=\frac{m}{ne^2\tau}=\frac{mv}{ne^2\lambda}=\frac{\sqrt{3mk_BT}}{ne^2\lambda}\propto\sqrt T$: the resistivity would grow like the square root of $T$.'),
    (r'(d) Phonon scattering.', r'Measured: $\rho\propto T$ around and above room temperature, not $\sqrt T$. Script 4.2.5: the electrons that carry current move with $v_F$, which does not depend on $T$; what changes is the number of phonons to scatter from. For $T\gg T_D$: $n(\hbar\omega)\approx k_BT/\hbar\omega\propto T$, so $\tau^{-1}\propto T$ and $\rho\propto T$ (eq. 4.26). For $T\ll T_D$: number of phonons $\propto T^3$, and each collision transfers only a small momentum, which costs another $T^2$: $\rho\propto T^5$ (eq. 4.29), on top of a constant residual resistivity from defects (eq. 4.32). So Drude’s picture gets both ingredients wrong: the speed is $v_F$, and the mean free path is not constant, $\lambda\propto1/T$ at high $T$.')],
   [r'$n=\rho_mN_A/M=8.5\times10^{28}$ m$^{-3}$', r'$\tau=m/(ne^2\rho)=2.5\times10^{-14}$ s', r'$\lambda=v\tau\approx3$ nm', r'constant $\lambda$: $\rho\propto\sqrt T$', r'phonons: $\rho\propto T$ ($T\gg T_D$), $T^5$ ($T\ll T_D$), plus residual resistivity'], source=OWN(9, 1)),
 Q(2, 'Positive and negative charge carriers', 'drift-corridor', ['ss-dri-L1', 'ss-dri-D1', 'ss-dri-c3', 'ss-sem-L1', 'ss-dri-Q1'],
   r'A 2D sample in the $xy$ plane with electrons (density $n_e$, scattering time $\tau_e$, charge $-e$) and holes ($n_h$, $\tau_h$, $+e$); field $E_x$ along $+x$, $B_z$ along $+z$; no net current in $y$. (a) Express $\mathbf J$ through the drift velocities. (b) Show $\mathbf v_e=-\mu_e(\mathbf E+\mathbf v_e\times\mathbf B)$, $\mathbf v_h=\mu_h(\mathbf E+\mathbf v_h\times\mathbf B)$; what are $\mu_e,\mu_h$? (c) For $E_x\gg|v_{e,y}|B_z,|v_{h,y}|B_z$ show $J_x=eE_x(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)$. (d) Show $J_y=eE_y(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)+eB_zE_x(n_e\mu_e^2-n_h\mu_h^2)$. What must $J_y$ be? (e) Show $E_y=J_xB_z\frac{n_h\mu_h^2-n_e\mu_e^2}{e(n_h\mu_h+n_e\mu_e)^2}$; extract $R_H=E_y/(J_xB_z)$ and discuss its sign.',
   [(r'(a) Current density.', r'$\mathbf J=-en_e\mathbf v_e+en_h\mathbf v_h$.'),
    (r'(b) Drift velocities.', r'Drude equation with the Lorentz force (script eq. 4.1), steady state $\dot{\mathbf v}=0$: electrons $\frac{m_e\mathbf v_e}{\tau_e}=-e(\mathbf E+\mathbf v_e\times\mathbf B)$, holes $\frac{m_h\mathbf v_h}{\tau_h}=+e(\mathbf E+\mathbf v_h\times\mathbf B)$. Hence the stated forms with the mobilities $\mu_e=\frac{e\tau_e}{m_e}$, $\mu_h=\frac{e\tau_h}{m_h}$ (both positive, script eq. 4.5).'),
    (r'(c) $J_x$.', r'$(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)_x=v_yB_z$. $v_{e,x}=-\mu_e(E_x+v_{e,y}B_z)\approx-\mu_eE_x$, $v_{h,x}\approx\mu_hE_x$. $J_x=-en_ev_{e,x}+en_hv_{h,x}=eE_x(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)$. The carriers drift in opposite directions but carry opposite charge, so both currents point along $+x$: conductivities add, $\sigma=e(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)$.'),
    (r'(d) $J_y$.', r'$(\mathbf v\times\mathbf B)_y=-v_xB_z$. $v_{e,y}=-\mu_e(E_y-v_{e,x}B_z)=-\mu_eE_y-\mu_e^2E_xB_z$; $v_{h,y}=\mu_h(E_y-v_{h,x}B_z)=\mu_hE_y-\mu_h^2E_xB_z$. Both kinds are deflected to the <b>same</b> side ($-y$). $J_y=-en_ev_{e,y}+en_hv_{h,y}=eE_y(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)+eB_zE_x(n_e\mu_e^2-n_h\mu_h^2)$. Boundary condition: $J_y=0$ (the individual $v_y$ are not zero: electrons and holes both flow to the same edge, where they recombine).'),
    (r'(e) Hall field and coefficient.', r'$J_y=0$: $E_y=E_xB_z\frac{n_h\mu_h^2-n_e\mu_e^2}{n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h}$; insert $E_x=\frac{J_x}{e(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)}$: $E_y=J_xB_z\frac{n_h\mu_h^2-n_e\mu_e^2}{e(n_h\mu_h+n_e\mu_e)^2}$, so $R_H=\frac{n_h\mu_h^2-n_e\mu_e^2}{e(n_h\mu_h+n_e\mu_e)^2}$. Checks: $n_h=0$ gives $-\frac1{n_ee}$ (script eq. 4.9), $n_e=0$ gives $+\frac1{n_he}$. The sign is set by $n\mu^2$, not by $n$ alone: a few very mobile carriers can outweigh many slow ones, and for $n_h\mu_h^2=n_e\mu_e^2$ the Hall voltage vanishes although current flows.')],
   [r'$\mathbf J=-en_e\mathbf v_e+en_h\mathbf v_h$', r'$\mu=e\tau/m$ from the steady-state Drude equation with Lorentz force', r'$\sigma=e(n_e\mu_e+n_h\mu_h)$: contributions add', r'$J_y=0$ although $v_{e,y},v_{h,y}\ne0$', r'$R_H=\frac{n_h\mu_h^2-n_e\mu_e^2}{e(n_h\mu_h+n_e\mu_e)^2}$, sign set by $n\mu^2$'], xp=60, source=OWN(9, 2)),
])
